# 3D Tic-Tac-Toe - ComS 4370 Project 2A
# Author - Dylan Topic - Dylan004@iastate.edu
3D Tic-Tac-Toe made with Unity for ComS 4370. The board is a 4x4x4 cube and 
you play against an AI that uses Minimax with alpha-beta pruning. Five 
difficulty levels.

---

## How to run

Open the project in Unity (Unity 6 or 2022.3 LTS or newer), open the scene 
at Assets/Scenes/MainScene.unity (or SampleScene.unity), and press Play. The 
game starts at Medium difficulty.

---

## Controls

- Left click - place your piece on a cell
- Right click + drag - orbit the camera around the board
- Scroll wheel - zoom in/out
- R - reset the camera view
- Difficulty buttons (top of screen) - switch difficulty, also starts a new game
- New Game button - restart at current difficulty

---

## How to play

You are X, the AI is O. Click any empty cell to place your piece. To win 
you need 4 in a row along any line through the cube. That includes regular 
rows and columns, vertical pillars, diagonals across each layer, diagonals 
on the vertical faces, and the 3D diagonals that go corner to corner 
through the middle of the cube. There are 76 winning lines total.

The status text at the top shows whose turn it is and the current 
difficulty. The AI thinks for a moment then plays automatically.

---

## Project structure

    Project2A_Minimax3DTicTacToe/
     Assets/
       Scripts/
         Board/
           BoardBuilder.cs       - spawns the 64 cell GameObjects from a prefab
           BoardState.cs         - pure data game state, [x,y,z] grid of Player values
           Cell.cs               - component on each cube storing its coordinates
           WinLine.cs            - struct for a single 4-cell winning line
           WinLineGenerator.cs   - generates all 76 winning lines programmatically
         AI/
           Minimax.cs            - minimax + alpha-beta pruning + heuristic
           Difficulty.cs         - enum mapping levels to depth and blunder chance
         Game/
           GameManager.cs        - bridges logical state and visuals, handles turns
           InputHandler.cs       - raycasts mouse clicks to select cells
           UIController.cs       - wires UI buttons and status text to GameManager
           OrbitCamera.cs        - right-click orbit camera controls
           Player.cs             - Player and ControllerType enums
       Prefabs/
         Cell.prefab             - the cube prefab used for every cell
       Materials/
         Mat_Empty.mat           - translucent gray, empty cells
         Mat_PlayerX.mat         - blue, X pieces
         Mat_PlayerO.mat         - red, O pieces
       Scenes/
         MainScene.unity         - the only scene

---

## The board

The board is 4 layers of 4x4 grids stacked vertically. Each cell is a Unity 
cube. Empty cells use a translucent gray material so you can see through 
them to the layers behind. When you place a piece the cube turns blue (X) 
or red (O).

The board is built at runtime by BoardBuilder. It instantiates 64 copies of 
the cell prefab, centers them around the origin, and stores them in a 3D 
array so GameManager can look them up by coordinates.

---

## Algorithm

I wrote Minimax and alpha-beta pruning from scratch, no asset packages. 
Here is what it does:

- recursively explores possible moves up to a depth limit
- alternates between maximizing (AI) and minimizing (opponent) layers
- alpha-beta pruning cuts off branches that cant affect the final decision
- move ordering tries center cells first since they show up in more winning 
  lines, which makes pruning kick in earlier
- at the depth limit it falls back to a heuristic evaluation function

The heuristic scores positions by looking at all 76 winning lines and 
adding up points based on how many AI pieces vs opponent pieces each line 
has. Lines with both players are dead so they get skipped. A 3-in-a-row 
with an empty cell is weighted way higher than a 2-in-a-row because thats 
an actual threat. Opponent line scores are slightly higher than AI scores 
at the same count which gives the AI a defensive bias.

There is also a small cell richness bonus for occupying cells that 
participate in more winning lines (interior cells are worth more than 
corners). Its just a tiebreaker.

---

## Difficulty levels

There are 5 levels mapped to Minimax search depth:

- Easy - depth 1, 50% chance to play a random move
- Casual - depth 2, 25% chance to play a random move
- Medium - depth 3, 5% chance to play a random move
- Hard - depth 4, no random moves
- Expert - depth 5, no random moves

I needed the random move chance at lower levels because the heuristic is 
strong enough that even depth 1 plays reasonably well if it always picks 
the best move. Adding blunders at low difficulties gives a smoother 
difficulty curve. Hard and Expert are pure Minimax with no randomness.

---

A few things I added that werent strictly required:

- Camera orbit controls so you can spin around the board and zoom in/out, 
  which makes the 3D layout much easier to read than a fixed camera
- The blunder chance system on lower difficulties for a smoother curve 
  instead of pure depth-based difficulty
- Move ordering for alpha-beta which makes Expert depth 5 actually feasible 
  to search through in reasonable time
- AI vs AI mode (set both player controllers to AI in the inspector) which 
  was useful for testing

---

## Notes

- the new Input System package is used, so Active Input Handling in Player 
  Settings needs to be on "Input System Package (New)" or "Both" with the 
  native input backends enabled
- Expert difficulty opening moves take a few seconds since depth 5 has a 
  lot to search, this is normal and gets faster as the board fills up
- RestartGame calls StopAllCoroutines first so the AI doesnt drop a piece 
  on a freshly reset board if you restart mid-move
- there is no multi-game scoring or progression, just one game at a time

